    faqs: [
        { title: 'What is the difference between a class and an object?', answer: 'A class is a blueprint or template that defines attributes and methods. An object is an instance of a class created at runtime with specific values. For example, Car is a class; myCar with color=red and speed=120 is an object.' },
        { title: 'How do design patterns help in software development?', answer: 'Design patterns provide proven solutions to recurring design problems. They improve code reusability, maintainability, and communication between developers. Common patterns include Singleton, Factory, Observer, and Strategy.' },
        { title: 'What is the purpose of version control?', answer: 'Version control tracks changes to source code over time, enables collaboration among developers, allows reverting to previous versions, and supports branching for parallel development. Git is the most widely used version control system.' },
        { title: 'How does continuous integration differ from continuous deployment?', answer: 'Continuous Integration (CI) automatically builds and tests code on every commit. Continuous Deployment (CD) automatically deploys every change that passes the pipeline to production. Continuous Delivery is similar but requires manual approval for production deployment.' },
        { title: 'What is technical debt and how should it be managed?', answer: 'Technical debt is the implied cost of future rework caused by choosing a quick solution now instead of a better approach. It should be tracked, prioritized, and reduced through regular refactoring sprints, code reviews, and automated quality checks.' },
        { title: 'When should I use microservices vs monolithic architecture?', answer: 'Use monolithic architecture for small teams, simple domains, and rapid prototyping. Use microservices for large teams, complex domains requiring independent deployment, and systems needing different scaling strategies per component.' },
        { title: 'What are the key principles of Clean Code?', answer: 'Clean Code principles include meaningful naming, small functions that do one thing, minimal comments (code should be self-documenting), proper error handling, DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself), and consistent formatting.' },
        { title: 'How do I write effective unit tests?', answer: 'Follow the AAA pattern: Arrange (setup), Act (execute), Assert (verify). Test one behavior per test. Use descriptive test names. Mock external dependencies. Aim for high coverage but focus on meaningful tests over coverage numbers.' },
        { title: 'What is the Agile Manifesto?', answer: 'The Agile Manifesto values individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan.' },
        { title: 'How do I handle merge conflicts in Git?', answer: 'Open the conflicted file, look for conflict markers (<<<, ===, >>>), choose the correct code or combine changes, remove the markers, stage the file, and commit. Use git mergetool for visual conflict resolution.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between REST and GraphQL?', answer: 'REST uses multiple endpoints with fixed data structures. GraphQL uses a single endpoint where clients specify exactly what data they need. REST is simpler; GraphQL reduces over-fetching and under-fetching but adds complexity.' },
        { title: 'How does Scrum differ from Kanban?', answer: 'Scrum uses fixed-length sprints with defined roles and ceremonies. Kanban uses continuous flow with WIP limits and no prescribed roles. Scrum is better for teams needing structure; Kanban suits teams wanting flexibility and continuous delivery.' },
        { title: 'What is the purpose of code reviews?', answer: 'Code reviews catch bugs, ensure code quality, share knowledge across the team, enforce coding standards, and mentor junior developers. Reviews should be constructive, focused, and timely. Use checklists for consistency.' },
        { title: 'What tools are used for static code analysis?', answer: 'Common tools include SonarQube (multi-language), Checkstyle (Java formatting), PMD (Java bug detection), SpotBugs (bytecode analysis), ESLint (JavaScript), and Pylint (Python). They detect code smells, bugs, and security vulnerabilities.' },
        { title: 'How do I estimate software project effort?', answer: 'Common techniques include Story Points with Planning Poker (relative estimation), Function Point Analysis (formal), COCOMO (algorithmic), expert judgment, and three-point estimation (optimistic, likely, pessimistic). Velocity tracking improves accuracy over time.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between functional and non-functional requirements?', answer: 'Functional requirements describe what the system should do (features, behavior). Non-functional requirements describe how the system should perform (quality attributes like performance, security, usability, reliability, scalability).' },
        { title: 'How does Docker differ from virtual machines?', answer: 'Docker containers share the host OS kernel, making them lightweight and fast to start. VMs include a full OS, providing stronger isolation but using more resources. Containers are ideal for microservices; VMs for full OS-level isolation.' },
        { title: 'What is the SOLID principle of Dependency Inversion?', answer: 'Dependency Inversion states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions. This enables flexible, testable architectures.' },
        { title: 'How do I implement proper error handling?', answer: 'Use specific exception types, catch at the appropriate level, provide meaningful error messages, log errors with context, use fail-fast for unrecoverable errors, and never swallow exceptions silently. In REST APIs, return proper HTTP status codes.' },
        { title: 'What is pair programming and when should it be used?', answer: 'Pair programming has two developers working together: a driver who writes code and a navigator who reviews. It is effective for complex problems, knowledge transfer, onboarding new team members, and reducing defects in critical code.' },
    ],
